13
00:00:32,435 --> 00:00:38,160
Start with the problem, then brainstorm causes across all six categories, before anyone is blamed.

14
00:00:47,129 --> 00:00:51,291
Map the journey from emergency department to ward, and watch the waiting appear.

15
00:00:51,291 --> 00:00:55,609
Find the bottleneck first, because speeding up one step can simply flood the next.

16
00:01:06,057 --> 00:01:08,525
Put the biggest causes first with a Pareto chart.

17
00:01:08,525 --> 00:01:11,497
Test your hypothesis, and see what the numbers really say.

18
00:01:24,986 --> 00:01:26,441
Plot your change over time.

19
00:01:26,441 --> 00:01:33,233
In this example, door to antibiotic time fell from eighty six to fifty four minutes, and the toolkit flags the shift for you.

20
00:01:33,233 --> 00:01:36,306
Safety comes from resilient systems, not perfect people.

21
00:01:43,914 --> 00:01:46,379
Control limits tell you when a spike is real.

22
00:01:46,379 --> 00:01:52,954
In the red bead ward, every point sits inside the limits: the differences come from the system, so stop ranking people.

23
